AMD FidelityFX Super Resolution 3.1 as a second backend of 0161's scene upscaler, beside DLSS: the same scene detection,
capture, depth (0163), camera motion vectors, jitter, pre-scene targets (0162, 0164) and write-back, with AMD's signed
amd_fidelityfx_dx12.dll (FidelityFX SDK v1.1.4, upscaler 3.1.4) through its C API, ffx_api. thirdparty/fidelityfx-api
holds that SDK's ffx_api.h, ffx_api_types.h, ffx_upscale.h and dx12/ffx_api_dx12.h unchanged, with AMD's MIT license.

d3d12_fsr3_mode (off, native, quality, balanced, performance, ultra_performance) selects it when d3d12_dlss_mode is off,
d3d12_fsr3_library names the DLL and d3d12_fsr3_sharpness (0 to 100, default 80) is FSR's own RCAS. Fsr3Upscaler loads
the DLL at the first scene draw (never unloaded), queries the render size of the mode and the jitter phase count with
AMD's helpers (scene_layout.h has the same formulas as fallbacks) and records the context's creation and each upscale
on the command list through ExternalCall. The context is created once, again only when the depth convention changes,
and destroyed after the GPU is done with it. The inputs keep the states FidelityFX is told (compute read, output UAV).

The jitter is AMD's sequence (Halton(2,3) of index + 1, minus a half) moving the scene by render pixels, x right and y
down, as AMD's projection jitter does; the motion vectors are render pixels from the current to the previous position
without jitter (motionVectorScale 1); depth is 0 to 1, inverted when the scene's zfunc is GREATER or GEQUAL. The camera
planes and vertical field of view come from the depth and y rows of the draws' object-to-clip matrices (the projection
most of them share), FLT_MAX for an infinite far plane. viewSpaceToMetersFactor is AMD's default of 1. Log lines of the
shared code carry the backend's name; DLSS's are unchanged.

FSR 3.1 writes alpha 1 to its whole output, and the game's passes after the scene read the scene's own alpha. The
write-back of the FSR output (scene_encode mode 3) keeps FSR 3.1's color and takes alpha from the scene's own
render-size color, sampled bilinearly at each output pixel's unjittered position.

